window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][2] = "<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Which partner is hardest for new PMs? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Often legal and privacy, because they get engaged late. The fix is to bring them in at the problem stage with the facts and ask what the requirement is, so it shapes the design instead of blocking the launch.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you build trust with a skeptical engineering lead? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By being reliable: honest dates, clear scope, answering questions fast, protecting their time from low-value requests, and crediting their ideas. Trust comes from a track record, so I'd start with a small change that goes well.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c01-04\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C01-04 · Intermediate · interview-craft · IC</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> You have about two years of experience. Why an associate role?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I want to learn, and I'm early in my career.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Describes the scope owned so far and the gap the role fills.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> States level fit honestly, with scope and evidence, what the associate level would let the candidate learn, why this route, and awareness of eligibility.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'm applying for an associate role because it matches the scope I've owned and the gap I want to close. In my current role I own the metrics for onboarding and have run two experiments and one launch, which raised completion by six points. What I haven't done is carry a roadmap through a quarter of competing priorities, decide between several teams' requests or lead a launch across functions; the associate level is where I'd do that with guidance, on a scope where I can deliver. I've read the eligibility on each posting and applied to roles that match my experience: direct associate and first-level PM roles and, where the wording allows, early-career programs. I'd rather join at the level where I'll be effective and learn than at a level where I would be stretched past my evidence.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Honest scope; a defined gap; eligibility awareness.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Wouldn't you be overqualified? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I don't think so: two years in a focused role is one piece of the job. What I haven't shown is ownership across functions and on a roadmap. If after a few months the scope feels too small, I'd ask for more, which is part of how an associate grows.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Would you consider a mid-level PM role? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;If the scope and the evidence matched, yes; but I'd want to see that my experience, with its limits, fits what the team needs. A level that I cannot yet evidence would not be good for me or for the team.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c01-05\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C01-05 · Intermediate · interview-craft · IC</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> What would you do in your first week as an APM?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd meet the team and learn the product.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names what to learn: users, metrics, partners, decisions.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> A concrete first-week plan: map decision rights, read the metrics and past decisions, meet each partner with specific questions, use the product as a user, find a small early contribution, and agree expectations with the manager.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'd aim to learn how decisions get made and what the numbers say, not to change anything. Day one: meet my manager and agree what success looks like in thirty, sixty and ninety days. Day two: use the product as a user for a few hours, read support tickets and the last quarter's roadmap and launch reviews. Day three: meet each partner, engineering, design, data, support and legal, with the same questions: what do you need from a PM, what has gone well and badly, and what should I know that isn't written down? Day four: look at the product's metrics, how they are defined and what moved last quarter. Day five: write a one-page summary of what I've learned, with questions, and share it with my manager; and I'd pick one small thing to own, such as a metric definition or a recurring report.</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd also map the team's decision rights early: who decides what, so I'm not surprised later. I wouldn't propose big changes in week one, since I'd lack the context to be right.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> A concrete plan; listening first; decision rights; humility.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if your manager gives you no direction? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd propose my own plan in the one-pager and ask for corrections. Most managers prefer a draft to an open question, and it shows initiative. I'd also ask a peer PM what they wish they had known.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What is the main mistake new PMs make? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Trying to prove themselves with a big idea before understanding the history. A good first win is small: a metric corrected, a doc that clarifies a decision, or a bug that was ignored.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c01-06\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C01-06 · Intermediate · interview-craft · IC</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> What is a common misconception about being a PM?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;That PMs are in charge.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names a misconception and corrects it with a reason.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Gives two or three misconceptions with evidence from the candidate's own work, and shows what the reality implies for how to prepare.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;Three. First, that a PM is in charge: a PM owns an outcome, not people, and everything depends on influence. In my work I never told an engineer what to build; I brought the problem and the evidence, and the plan came from the conversation. Second, that the job is generating ideas: ideas are cheap; the work is choosing, measuring and saying no. When I had four requests and one engineer, the value was in the written tradeoff and the no. Third, that you need to code: you need to understand how things work well enough to ask good questions and judge tradeoffs, which I learned by asking engineers to explain our system and by reading our architecture notes, but I don't write production code. The implication for preparing is to practice decisions, evidence and communication more than frameworks or brainstorming, which is how I've spent my time.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Specific corrections tied to experience; implications for practice.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Which misconception did you hold yourself? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;That a good product idea would speak for itself. I learned that an idea without a problem statement, evidence and a tradeoff is ignored. It changed how I write: I now lead with the problem and the number.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Is it true that PMs are overpaid for what they do? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I wouldn't engage with the premise as stated. The role carries accountability for outcomes without authority, which is hard, and the value is in the decisions that prevent building the wrong thing. I'd rather talk about what good looks like than compare pay.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c01-07\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C01-07 · Hard · interview-craft · IC</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> A rotational program offer and a direct first-level PM offer arrive in the same week. How do you decide?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd take the more prestigious one.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Lists criteria such as learning, scope and pay.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Defines criteria and weights, gathers information from both (team, manager, scope, rotations, conversion), compares with a simple scored table, considers reversibility and risk, and decides with a stated reversal condition.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'd decide with criteria, and get information first. Criteria that matter to me: the scope and ownership I would have in the first year; the quality of the manager and team; learning rate (breadth against depth); the path after the program or role (conversion to a PM role, mobility); the people I'd work with; location and work authorization; and compensation. I'd ask each company concrete questions: in the program, how team matching works, how rotations are chosen and what share convert to PM roles and where; in the direct role, what I'd own, who I'd report to and how performance is judged.</p>\n<p>&quot;Then a simple scored comparison. Suppose I weight scope 25%, manager and team 25%, learning rate 20%, path 15%, compensation 10% and location 5%, and score each one to five: the program might score scope 3, manager 3 (unknown until matching), learning 5, path 4, compensation 4, location 3; the direct role scope 5, manager 4, learning 3, path 3, compensation 4, location 4. The weighted totals are 3.65 and 3.90 (invented), close enough that the uncertain items (the program's team matching, the direct role's growth path) decide it. I'd resolve those with calls to current employees. I'd note reversibility: a direct role can be left later; a program's value is partly the cohort and the early rotations. The decision I'd make, and what would change it: if the program could tell me my first team and it was strong, I'd lean toward the program; if not, the direct role with a clear scope.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Criteria and weights; information gathering; a scored table with uncertainty; reversal conditions.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if one company pressures you to decide quickly? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd ask for a few more days and give a reason, and tell the other company I have an offer and a date. Reasonable employers extend deadlines; one that does not tells me something about the culture.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you handle a counteroffer? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd treat it as information about how the company values me and about what is negotiable, but base the decision on the same criteria. Negotiation is outside this book and planned for the Second Edition; I'd get advice from a mentor before deciding.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c01-08\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C01-08 · Hard · interview-craft · IC</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Three months into an associate PM job, you realize the work is mostly scheduling and tracking, not product decisions. What do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd quit,&quot; or &quot;I'd wait and see.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Checks the facts and talks to the manager.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates a temporary phase from a structural mismatch, gathers evidence on decisions and ownership, proposes a specific change in scope, uses decision rights to define the gap, sets a time-boxed plan, and considers other options if it does not change.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;First I'd check whether I'm right. I'd write down, for the last month, which decisions I made, which I influenced and which I only recorded; if the share of decisions about what to build and why is near zero, the mismatch is real. I'd also ask whether it is a phase (a launch crunch needing coordination) or the design of the role.</p>\n<p>&quot;Then I'd talk to my manager with the evidence, not a complaint: 'Here is what I've spent my time on, and here is where I think I can add more: I'd like to own the metric for onboarding and propose the next two experiments.' I'd map the team's decision rights with them to find a decision I can own. I'd propose a specific, small change in scope with a date to review, for example taking the metric and the roadmap input for one surface within the next quarter, and I'd keep delivering the coordination work well, because that earns trust.</p>\n<p>&quot;If after a quarter nothing changes, I'd look for another route: a different team in the same company (an internal transfer), or another company's direct associate role, using what I've learned about the role. I'd decide on the evidence and the date, not on frustration.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Evidence before complaint; a specific proposal; a time-boxed plan; options.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if your manager says that this is how the role works for everyone at your level? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Then I'd ask what the path to owning decisions is and how long it takes for people on this team, and what the evidence is from the people who made it. If the path is real and a year or more, I'd weigh it against alternatives. I wouldn't assume bad faith; roles differ.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Could this have been avoided? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Partly: I'd read the duties and ask in the interview what the associate owns, who decides what, and what the last associate worked on. Those questions reveal the real role, and I'd ask them before accepting an offer.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c01-12-exercises\"></span>\n<h3>12. Exercises</h3>\n<p>Eight exercises. Each has a model solution and an alternative. Every figure is invented.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c01-01\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C01-01 · A decision-rights map</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> For a feature of your choice (or &quot;add a share button to a photo app&quot;), fill in the decision-rights map (FW-C01-01) for six decisions.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Role understanding.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Eight minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C01-01 and Worked example 3.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> For the share button: (1) <em>Whether to build it now:</em> PM decides; consulted data, design, engineering. (2) <em>Success metric (shares per active user, with recipient sign-ups as a guardrail):</em> PM; consulted data. (3) <em>Placement and interaction:</em> design; consulted PM, engineering. (4) <em>How sharing works technically (links, permissions):</em> engineering; consulted PM, privacy. (5) <em>Privacy and consent of recipients:</em> privacy and legal decide the requirement; PM consults early. (6) <em>Launch to all:</em> PM with leadership; informed support and marketing. State your role for each: own, influence, or consult.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a column &quot;what could go wrong if this isn't clear.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Writing it down takes minutes and prevents days of conflict.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c01-02\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C01-02 · A partner map</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Fill in the partner map (FW-C01-02) for your own team or a team you know: five partners, their job, what each needs from you and how you work together.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Interview preparation.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Your experience.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Eight minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C01-02 and IQ-C01-03.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> An example: <em>Engineering lead:</em> builds and owns technical risk; needs stable scope and honest priorities; we agree scope at the start of each sprint and I send changes as written requests. <em>Designer:</em> shapes the experience; needs the user problem and evidence; I share research notes before design starts and ask for their options. <em>Data scientist:</em> analysis and experiments; needs a precise question and agreed metrics; we write the metric definition together before the test. <em>Legal and privacy:</em> requirements; need facts early; I involve them at the problem stage. <em>My manager:</em> direction and resources; needs the answer first and bad news early; a weekly four-line update. Add a specific change you made for one of them.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a partner you have neglected and a plan to engage them.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A detailed map takes an hour and prevents surprises.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c01-03\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C01-03 · Classify six job snippets</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Classify each as product manager, program manager, product owner, product operations, analyst or product marketing, and say what you looked at: (a) &quot;Own the roadmap for payments; define success metrics; partner with engineering and design&quot;; (b) &quot;Maintain and groom the backlog; accept stories; attend ceremonies&quot;; (c) &quot;Plan cross-team launches; track dependencies and risks; report status&quot;; (d) &quot;Build dashboards; analyze experiments; present insights to PMs&quot;; (e) &quot;Define positioning and messaging; run launch campaigns&quot;; (f) &quot;Create processes and tooling for PMs; standardize launch reviews.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Reading postings.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> C01.3.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) Product manager: owns a roadmap and outcome, defines metrics. (b) Product owner: backlog and acceptance. (c) Program manager: delivery, dependencies, risks. (d) Analyst: analysis for others' decisions. (e) Product marketing: positioning and launch. (f) Product operations: processes and tooling for PMs. In each case the duty verbs decide it: owns the roadmap and defines success (PM), maintains the backlog (owner), tracks dependencies (program), analyzes (analyst), positions (marketing), standardizes processes (operations).</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Rewrite (b) so that it would be a PM duty (&quot;decide which problems the backlog should address and why&quot;).</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Reading duties takes a minute and avoids misapplication.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c01-04\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C01-04 · An eligibility check</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> You graduated in October 2024 and have two years of experience. Apply the fit check (FW-C01-03) to three postings: (A) &quot;Recent graduates only; graduated within the past 12 months&quot;; (B) &quot;1 to 4 years of experience in product, analytics or engineering&quot;; (C) &quot;0 to 2 years; graduates and early-career; technical degree or equivalent experience.&quot; Give a fit rating for each and what you would do.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Applications.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C01-03 and Worked example 2.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (A) Skip: the eligibility excludes you; do not change the dates. (B) Apply: matches your experience; check the duties. (C) Apply with care: your two years are at the edge (&quot;0 to 2&quot;); check whether &quot;technical degree or equivalent experience&quot; fits, and ask the recruiter whether two full years is within the range. Add (B) and (C) to the plan, ask internal contacts about openings, and keep (A)-type programs on a watch list for cohorts with different wording.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Message the recruiter for (C): &quot;I graduated in October 2024 with about two years of product experience; is that within the eligible range?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Asking can feel awkward and prevents a wasted application.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c01-05\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C01-05 · Your reading plan</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> You have 60 hours. Your strengths (1 to 5) on the ten competencies, in the order of C01.6, are 4, 3, 2, 3, 3, 2, 3, 4, 3, 2; importance is 3, 2, 3, 3, 3, 2, 2, 2, 2, 3. Compute hours by competency and name the chapters for the top two.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Preparation planning.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Gap = 5 − strength.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Seven minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C01-04 and Worked example 4.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Gaps: 1, 2, 3, 2, 2, 3, 2, 1, 2, 3. Importance × gap: 3, 4, 9, 6, 6, 6, 4, 2, 4, 9; the total is 53. Hours: 60 × each ÷ 53: product sense 3.4, discovery 4.5, problem solving 10.2, analytics 6.8, execution 6.8, technical 6.8, AI 4.5, design and UX 2.3, strategy 4.5, behavioral 10.2. The top two are problem solving (C02, C03, C04) and behavioral and communication (C31, C32), each about 10 hours. Re-score after a mock in four weeks.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Set a floor of 3 hours for every competency and scale the rest.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A formula guides; judgment adjusts for rounds with veto risks.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c01-06\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C01-06 · A 90-second role description</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Write and say aloud a 90-second answer to &quot;What does a product manager do?&quot; using the structure in section 4, with a real example from your own work.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The opening of a screen.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Your experience.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Eight minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Section 4 and IQ-C01-01.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> &quot;A PM owns the outcome of a product or feature: deciding what is worth building and why, defining success and making the launch call, with engineering, design and data, who decide how it is built, how it feels and what the data means, so influence matters more than authority. For example, I own onboarding metrics at my company; I found that 38% of new users dropped at the permissions step, proposed moving the request later, agreed the scope with the engineering lead and design, and tested it: completion rose from 52% to 58% (invented). That's the part I enjoy most, deciding what to change with evidence, and I'd like to do it with a bigger scope.&quot; Replace the example with your own, true and numbered, and time it.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Lead with the example if the interviewer asks &quot;what did you do?&quot; and add the definition after.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A shorter answer leaves less to probe; keep one example you can expand.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c01-07\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C01-07 · Debunk three misconceptions</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> For three misconceptions from C01.7, write the reality in one sentence and one piece of evidence from your own work or from a specific, public example.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Interview preparation.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Your experience.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> C01.7 and IQ-C01-06.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Example for &quot;a PM is the CEO of the product&quot;: <em>Reality:</em> a PM owns the outcome and influences the people who build it. <em>Evidence:</em> &quot;I never told an engineer what to build; I agreed scope with the engineering lead after showing the drop at one step and asking what it would cost.&quot; For &quot;PMs generate ideas&quot;: <em>Reality:</em> the work is choosing, measuring and saying no. <em>Evidence:</em> &quot;I had four requests and one engineer; the written tradeoff and the no were the value.&quot; For &quot;PMs must code&quot;: <em>Reality:</em> technical fluency, not production code. <em>Evidence:</em> &quot;I asked an engineer to explain our caching design and used it to judge a request's cost; I don't write code.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Choose misconceptions you once held yourself and say what changed your mind.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Specific evidence makes the answer credible and personal.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c01-08\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C01-08 · Ask the questions that reveal the real role</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Write five questions to ask a hiring manager at the end of an interview for an associate PM role, whose answers would reveal whether the role is product management or something closer to program management or analysis.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The end of a round (C33.12).</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> C01.3, FW-C01-01 and IQ-C01-08.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (1) &quot;What would I own in the first six months: a metric, a feature or a roadmap?&quot; (2) &quot;Which decisions would I make myself, and which would I propose to someone else?&quot; (3) &quot;What did the last associate on this team work on, and what did they ship?&quot; (4) &quot;How do you decide what the team builds, and where does a PM's input come in?&quot; (5) &quot;How is an associate's performance judged: outcomes of the product, delivery of the plan or something else?&quot; Listen for specifics (a named metric, a shipped feature, decisions owned); vague answers about &quot;supporting&quot; the team suggest a narrower role. Follow up on one answer in your next conversation.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a question to a peer PM: &quot;What does a typical week look like for you, and how much of it is writing and decisions?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Pointed questions can feel bold; asked politely, they show seriousness and protect you from a mismatch.</p>\n<span id=\"c01-13-practice-protocol\"></span>\n<h3>13. Practice protocol</h3>\n<p><strong>Timed drills (three a week for two weeks).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Week 1: describe the role.</em> Use IQ-C01-01, IQ-C01-02, IQ-C01-03, EX-C01-01, EX-C01-02 and EX-C01-06. Record yourself giving the 90-second role description three times, and fill in your decision-rights and partner maps.</li>\n<li><em>Week 2: fit and plan.</em> Use IQ-C01-04, IQ-C01-05, IQ-C01-07, EX-C01-03, EX-C01-04 and EX-C01-05. Apply the fit check to five real postings, compute your reading plan from a scored mock, and write your first-week plan.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>With a partner.</strong> Ask your partner to interrupt your role description with &quot;So you're like the CEO?&quot; and &quot;How is that different from a program manager?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Feedback checklist (yes, partly, no).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Did I describe the role in terms of decisions, partners and outcomes?</li>\n<li>Did I state decision rights accurately, and say where I influence?</li>\n<li>Did I distinguish the role from its neighbors?</li>\n<li>Did I give a concrete example with a number?</li>\n<li>Did I check eligibility, level, duties and window for each posting?</li>\n<li>Did I represent my experience and dates truthfully?</li>\n<li>Did I base my reading plan on importance times gap from a scored mock?</li>\n<li>Did I check application windows and deadlines this week?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer journal.</strong> Start it now (C34.5): the date, the prompt, the time used, the score, the one fix and the retest date.</p>\n<span id=\"c01-14-implementation-playbook\"></span>\n<h3>14. Implementation playbook</h3>\n<p>Use the first week of preparation to set up the plan and the facts.</p>\n<p><strong>Days 1 to 3.</strong> Read C01 and the first two parts of C02, C03 and C05. Write your decision-rights map and partner map for your current team. Find the careers pages of your target companies and note each program's window, eligibility wording and location and authorization lines.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Apply the fit check (FW-C01-03) to every posting you are considering; drop those whose eligibility excludes you; add direct associate and first-level roles and internal options. Run a first scored mock (C34) to get your strengths, and build your reading plan (FW-C01-04).</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Start the answer journal. Write and rehearse your 90-second role description with a true example. Set the weekly rhythm: reading, timed practice, a partner mock, the journal. Check deadlines weekly.</p>\n<p><strong>Month 1 and after.</strong> Follow the plan and re-score monthly; C37 will turn it into a twelve-month plan with a readiness checklist and a ninety-day plan for the first job.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliverables that become assets:</strong> the decision-rights map, the partner map, the posting fit table, the reading plan and the journal. Keep every claim about your experience true and dated.</p>\n<span id=\"c01-15-key-takeaways-and-further-reading\"></span>\n<h3>15. Key takeaways and further reading</h3>\n<span id=\"c01-key-takeaways\"></span>\n<h4>Key takeaways</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>A product manager owns an outcome and decides the what and why, and influences engineering, design, data and others, who decide the how, the experience and the evidence; decision rights explain most conflicts, and communication is most of the week.</li>\n<li>Large product organizations work in small feature teams built around a product triad (PM, engineering lead, design lead) with data, research, program management, product marketing and legal attached, and each partner needs something specific from the PM.</li>\n<li>Roles near product management differ in their core responsibility (program managers deliver, product owners run the backlog, analysts inform, product marketers position); read duties, not titles.</li>\n<li>Entry routes include rotational programs with fixed windows and strict eligibility, direct associate or first-level PM roles, internships and internal transfers; with about two years of experience, direct associate roles and internal moves are usually the best fit, and you must represent your dates and experience truthfully.</li>\n<li>Loops score ten competencies in three bands (Baseline, Strong, Standout); use the reading-and-practice plan (importance times gap), practice by producing answers, keep an answer journal and expect selective loops to reject many strong candidates.</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c01-further-reading\"></span>\n<h4>Further reading</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Marty Cagan, <em>Inspired</em> (2017), on how strong product teams work and the PM's role <code>[V: 2nd edition, Wiley, December 2017, ISBN 9781119387503]</code>.</li>\n<li>Melissa Perri, <em>Escaping the Build Trap</em> (2018), on outcome-focused product management <code>[V: O'Reilly, November 2018, ISBN 9781491973790]</code>.</li>\n<li>The careers pages and program pages of each company you are targeting (for example Google's APM program page, Meta's student programs page), read directly and with the date in mind <code>[V: Google Careers posting and Meta careers page, read October 2026]</code>.</li>\n<li>Appendix C (the competency matrix), for the full ten competencies with band behaviors.</li>\n<li>Chapters C02, C03, C05, C07 (foundations), C31 and C32 (communication), C33 (loops and company lenses) and C34 (practice system) of this book, and C37 (the twelve-month plan).</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c02\"></span>\n<h2>C02 — Product Thinking and Decision Foundations</h2>\n<span id=\"c02-1-what-interviewers-test\"></span>\n<h3>1. What interviewers test</h3>\n<p>Every product interview, whatever its label, tests one thing underneath: can you move from an open-ended situation to a decision, and can you show what that decision costs? The label changes (product sense, execution, strategy, behavioral), but the scorer is listening for the same few signals.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it shows up.</strong> <code>Inferred</code> from the round structure candidates commonly report: explicit tradeoff questions (&quot;ship now or polish?&quot;), &quot;how would you decide whether to build X?&quot;, why-probes into a past decision (&quot;why that option and not the other?&quot;), and silently in every design or execution answer, where the interviewer notes whether you chose and justified or merely listed. On the job, the same skill appears in your first month: feature requests from sales, a bug the CEO noticed, two engineers who disagree, a deadline that moved.</p>\n<p><strong>What is scored.</strong> The attributes companies state publicly point the same way. Google describes hiring on general cognitive ability, role-related knowledge, leadership and &quot;Googleyness&quot; <code>Public</code> <code>[V: summaries citing Google's how-we-hire page; Google re:Work structured-interviewing guide]</code>, and structured reasoning to a decision is how the first two are observed in a product round <code>Inferred</code>. Amazon publishes its Leadership Principles, including Are Right, A Lot, Bias for Action and Dive Deep <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Amazon's Leadership Principles page, amazon.jobs]</code>. A scorer's written note usually reads like &quot;chose an approach and defended it&quot; or &quot;listed options, never committed&quot; <code>Inferred</code>.</p>\n<p>Five observable signals, which the rest of the chapter trains:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You state the decision and the goal before you generate options.</li>\n<li>You separate the problem from the proposed solution.</li>\n<li>You name what each option costs, in a sentence that begins &quot;we get X by giving up Y.&quot;</li>\n<li>You say how reversible the decision is and size your evidence accordingly.</li>\n<li>You say what evidence would change your mind.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The failure this prevents.</strong> Four patterns lose candidates and, later, new PMs: solution-first answers (&quot;add a chatbot&quot;), indecision dressed as analysis (&quot;it depends on many factors&quot;), precision theater (a score of 7.3 with no stated basis), and options with no cost attached, which signals that the cost was never looked for.</p>\n<span id=\"c02-2-the-concept-from-first-principles\"></span>\n<h3>2. The concept from first principles</h3>\n<span id=\"c02-c02-1-product-thinking-defined\"></span>\n<h4>C02.1 Product thinking defined</h4>\n<p><strong>Product thinking</strong> is reasoning from a user problem to a solution to a measurable outcome, rather than starting from a solution. <a href=\"#gl-product-thinking\">product thinking</a> has four moves, always in this order: who has the problem and in what situation; why it matters, stated as an outcome you could measure; what options exist, including doing nothing; and what evidence would tell you which option works.</p>\n<p>Why it works: solutions are cheap to generate and expensive to build, and the problem is the constraint that prunes the option space. A team that starts from &quot;add a chatbot&quot; has already spent its most valuable resource, the freedom to choose a cheaper fix. A team that starts from &quot;customers who cannot find the return policy contact support three times more often&quot; can consider a link, a search tweak, a chatbot, or a policy change, and compare them.</p>\n<p>Two companion ideas are owned by later chapters and linked here rather than redefined. A <a href=\"#gl-problem-statement\">problem statement</a> is the written form of the first two moves (C06). <a href=\"#gl-jobs-to-be-done\">jobs to be done</a> is a lens for the first move, describing the progress a user is trying to make (C06).</p>\n<p><strong>When not to use it.</strong> Product thinking has a cost: the time spent framing. Skip the full chain when the decision is cheap and reversible and the need is obvious (fixing a typo, correcting a misleading label), when a legal or contractual obligation fixes the requirement, or when you are copying a pattern with proven value in your context, such as a standard password-reset flow.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> A team spends three weeks framing a one-line copy change on a checkout button. The decision is reversible in minutes and measurable the same day, so the correct process is to ship and watch the metric. Product thinking applied to a cheap, reversible choice is waste, and an interviewer will notice if you apply the same heavy structure to every prompt regardless of stakes.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Alternatives.</strong> Solution-first work is legitimate when you hold strong prior evidence from the same context, and in discovery sprints where building is the research method. The risk is that you will not notice when the evidence stops applying.</p>\n<p><strong>Hidden risks and second-order effects.</strong> The chain can be turned into a justification device: you pick the solution first, then write the problem so it fits. A tell is that the problem statement contains the solution's nouns (&quot;users need a pause button&quot;). A second-order effect of good framing is that it makes requests visible as symptoms, which changes how you talk to requesters; requesters hear a refusal of the feature as a refusal of the need unless you restate the need.</p>\n<span id=\"c02-c02-2-first-principles-reasoning-and-when-analogy-is-better\"></span>\n<h4>C02.2 First-principles reasoning and when analogy is better</h4>\n<p><strong>First-principles reasoning</strong> is breaking a problem into its basic facts and rebuilding the answer from them instead of copying an existing solution. <a href=\"#gl-first-principles-reasoning\">first-principles reasoning</a> has a procedure, not a mood:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>State the goal in terms of an outcome.</li>\n<li>List the facts that are fixed: physics, law, measured user behavior, hard cost floors.</li>\n<li>List the assumptions that are only conventions: &quot;onboarding means a training session&quot;, &quot;pricing is per seat&quot;, &quot;we must support every device&quot;.</li>\n<li>Rebuild a solution from the fixed facts, and challenge each convention you keep.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Analogy, copying what works elsewhere, is faster and usually right in well-trodden problems. Use analogy when the domain is mature and your context resembles the source. Use first principles when a constraint has changed (a technology made something cheap that used to be expensive), when two analogies give opposite answers, or when you suspect an inherited convention is the real bottleneck.</p>\n<p><strong>Hidden risks.</strong> You can mistake a convention for a fact, or a fact for a convention. A team that re-derives a payment flow from scratch and treats card-network rules as negotiable will fail late and expensively. You can also spend a week re-deriving what is already known. First principles is a tool for the part of the problem where analogies fail, not a replacement for knowing the field.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> A candidate asked to design an identity-verification step &quot;from first principles&quot; ignores that regulators in many markets prescribe what must be collected. The fixed-facts list was incomplete, so the elegant redesign was not allowed. Before you drop a convention, ask whether a rule you have not met enforces it.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c02-c02-3-tradeoffs-the-common-axes-and-how-to-make-them-visible\"></span>\n<h4>C02.3 Tradeoffs: the common axes and how to make them visible</h4>\n<p>A <strong>tradeoff</strong> is a choice in which gaining on one dimension costs something on another, stated with what is given up. <a href=\"#gl-tradeoff\">tradeoff</a> is the heart of PM judgment because resources are limited and users, the business and engineering rarely want the same thing.</p>\n<p>The axes that recur in product work:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Axis</th>\n<th>Typical form</th>\n<th>Note</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Speed versus quality</td>\n<td>Ship in two weeks versus six</td>\n<td>Often hides the real variable: which defects, for whom</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scope versus time</td>\n<td>Fewer features, earlier</td>\n<td>Cut scope before cutting quality when you can</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Short term versus long term</td>\n<td>Growth tactic now, trust cost later</td>\n<td>Most <a href=\"#gl-goodharts-law\">Goodhart's law</a> damage lives here (C05)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Breadth versus depth</td>\n<td>Support many cases versus one case well</td>\n<td>Relevant to every first release</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Simplicity versus power</td>\n<td>Fewer controls versus configurability</td>\n<td>Different users sit at different points</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Growth versus trust</td>\n<td>Aggressive prompts versus user goodwill</td>\n<td>Trust is slow to rebuild</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Privacy versus personalization</td>\n<td>Less data versus better relevance</td>\n<td>Often a design problem, not a pure tradeoff</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Consistency versus flexibility</td>\n<td>One way versus per-team variation</td>\n<td>Platform and design-system decisions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Build versus buy versus partner</td>\n<td>Control versus speed and cost</td>\n<td>Owned in depth by C18.7 (the chooser) and C21.10 (the technical side)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cost versus performance</td>\n<td>Cheaper infrastructure versus latency</td>\n<td>Frequent in AI features (C24)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Making a tradeoff visible.</strong> Put the sacrifice in the sentence. &quot;We recommend shipping the simplified checkout, which gives up saved-address editing for the first release.&quot; Three habits help: write what you give up before what you gain; name who bears the cost; and say whether the cost is reversible. A choice with no stated cost is unfinished.</p>\n<p><strong>The bad-decision drill.</strong> Before committing, ask: &quot;What would have to be true for this to be a bad decision?&quot; Each answer is either a risk to monitor, a condition that changes your recommendation, or a fact you can check now.</p>\n<p><strong>False tradeoffs.</strong> Some &quot;speed versus quality&quot; pairs dissolve when you find the actual cause. If the slow part is a review queue, fixing the queue buys speed without cutting quality. Test every either-or by asking what specifically makes the fast option worse, and whether that can be addressed separately.</p>\n<span id=\"c02-c02-4-decision-tools-reversibility-cost-of-delay-confidence-\"></span>\n<h4>C02.4 Decision tools: reversibility, cost of delay, confidence, why now</h4>\n<p><strong>Reversibility.</strong> A <strong>reversible decision</strong> is one that can be undone cheaply, so it can be made quickly on less evidence. <a href=\"#gl-reversible-decision\">reversible decision</a> is the single most useful classifier because it sets how much evidence you should demand. Changing button text, running a one-week test, or enabling a <a href=\"#gl-feature-flag\">feature flag</a> for 5% of users is reversible. Retiring a public API that customers integrated against, changing a price that customers have already seen, or shipping a hardware design that goes to manufacturing is hard or impossible to undo. Reversibility is a matter of degree and of audience: a change may be technically reversible while the reputational cost of reversing it is high (see CS-C02-02).</p>\n<p><strong>Cost of delay.</strong> The <strong>cost of delay</strong> is the value lost for each unit of time a decision or release is postponed. <a href=\"#gl-cost-of-delay\">cost of delay</a> puts a price on waiting. A fix that recovers revenue worth 1% of weekly sales loses that amount every week it sits in the backlog. Use it to resist the common pattern of making cheap decisions slowly. It also exposes decisions that feel urgent but cost little to delay.</p>\n<p><strong>Confidence levels.</strong> State how sure you are and what would raise or lower it. A label (low, medium, high) with a reason beats a false number: &quot;medium confidence, because we have usage data but no interviews with the affected segment.&quot; If you give a percentage, say it is a judgment. Tie confidence to the cost of being wrong: low confidence is acceptable on a reversible decision and a reason to gather evidence or stage the decision when it is not.</p>\n<p><strong>The why-now test.</strong> Ask what has changed that makes this the right time. Answers include a new capability, a competitor move, a metric trend, a dependency that cleared, or a deadline. A decision with no why-now is usually a preference and can wait.</p>\n<p><strong>When not to use these tools.</strong> Do not run a formal classification for decisions you can reverse in minutes; the time you spend classifying exceeds the cost of being wrong. Do not treat confidence labels as precision; they are communication aids. Do not use cost of delay as a reason to ship something unready; delay cost is one input beside the cost of error.</p>\n<span id=\"c02-c02-6-taste-and-judgment\"></span>\n<h4>C02.6 Taste and judgment</h4>\n<p><strong>Taste</strong> here means a trained ability to recognize quality and fit with a user's need quickly, before data arrives. <strong>Judgment</strong> means choosing well when evidence is incomplete. Neither is innate <code>Inferred</code>. Both develop through the same loop: exposure to many examples with explicit analysis, predictions made before outcomes are known, and honest comparison of the two.</p>\n<p>Four drills for building both:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The ten-minute teardown.</strong> Pick one screen you use. Write three things it does well and three it does badly, each with the user need it serves or fails. Reasons, not preferences: &quot;the primary button is below the fold on small phones, so the main action needs a scroll&quot; rather than &quot;it feels cluttered.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>The prediction log.</strong> Before an experiment readout or launch, write your prediction and your confidence. After the result, score it. Over a quarter you learn where your intuition is reliable and where it is not.</li>\n<li><strong>The decision journal.</strong> For each non-trivial decision, record the context, the options, your choice, your confidence and what would change your mind. Review after the outcome, separating decision quality from outcome luck.</li>\n<li><strong>Remove one thing.</strong> Take a product you know and say what you would remove and why. Removal forces a view of what matters.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What good looks like.</strong> You can give a reason for a judgment, you can say when you are likely to be wrong, and you change your mind quickly when evidence arrives.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> Strong taste misleads when you are not the user. A designer who loves a dense, keyboard-driven interface will misjudge a product for first-time smartphone users. Taste is a hypothesis about someone else's experience until you have checked it (the <a href=\"#gl-say-do-gap\">say-do gap</a>, C06).</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c02-c02-7-templates-decision-memo-problem-definition-pre-mortem\"></span>\n<h4>C02.7 Templates: decision memo, problem definition, pre-mortem</h4>\n<p>Three short templates put the habits of this chapter on the page. The <strong>decision memo</strong> (FW-C02-06) records a decision so that it can be reviewed later: the question, the options, the choice, the reasoning and what would change it. The <strong>pre-mortem</strong> (FW-C02-05) lists how a plan could fail before it starts. The third is a four-line <strong>problem definition</strong>, which stops a team from solving a problem nobody stated:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>Who</em> has the problem, and roughly how many people.</li>\n<li><em>What happens</em> today, described as something observable, with no solution in it.</li>\n<li><em>The cost</em>, as a number or a named consequence, and the evidence for it.</li>\n<li><em>What we do not know yet</em>, and the cheapest way to find out.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For example: &quot;New users who sign up on a phone abandon the app in the first session 55% of the time (invented); recordings show most stop at the permission prompts. We do not yet know whether the prompts or the empty home screen matter more; a five-user test would tell us.&quot; C06 extends this form into a full problem statement with evidence from discovery (<a href=\"#gl-problem-statement\">problem statement</a>). Use the templates when a decision will be revisited or shared. For a quick, reversible choice, one sentence does the job.</p>\n<span id=\"c02-3-frameworks\"></span>\n<h3>3. Frameworks</h3>\n<p>Six frameworks, set out below. The first three are working tools for any prompt; the last three structure decisions and documents.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c02-01\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C02-01 · The product thinking chain · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a request, idea or metric target, plus whatever you can observe about the people behind it.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a <a href=\"#gl-problem-statement\">problem statement</a> (C06), a measurable outcome, an option set that includes doing nothing, and an evidence plan.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the request has a need behind it that you can identify; you can reach or observe the users involved.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> time spent framing versus building; risk of over-framing cheap decisions.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> writing the problem to fit a chosen solution; endless why-chains with no decision; treating the requester as the user; skipping the &quot;do nothing&quot; option.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> a meal-kit app is asked for a &quot;pause subscription&quot; button. The chain converts it to &quot;subscribers with variable schedules cancel because skipping a week needs a support call,&quot; sets 60-day retention of that segment as the outcome, and generates four options. See Worked example 1.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The chain in steps: (1) restate the request as a need (&quot;what is this person trying to do?&quot;); (2) identify the <a href=\"#gl-user-segment\">user segment</a> and the situation; (3) write the outcome as a <a href=\"#gl-metric\">metric</a> with a direction and a window; (4) list options of different kinds, including a smaller one and doing nothing; (5) state the evidence you have, the evidence you lack, and the cheapest way to get it.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem-definition template</strong> (an input to the chain, expanded in C06):</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Field</th>\n<th>Prompt</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Who</td>\n<td>Which <a href=\"#gl-user-segment\">user segment</a>, in what situation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What happens today</td>\n<td>The observable behavior or friction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Why it matters</td>\n<td>The outcome affected, as a <a href=\"#gl-metric\">metric</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evidence</td>\n<td>What you know, and how well</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Not the problem</td>\n<td>The solution or feature the request named</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c02-02\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C02-02 · First-principles decomposition · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a goal stated as an outcome; the current solution and its conventions.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> two lists (fixed facts and inherited conventions) and a rebuilt design that challenges each convention.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you can distinguish what is physically, legally or empirically fixed from what is merely customary; you will check any &quot;fixed&quot; item you are unsure of.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> deeper insight versus time; risk of reinventing known solutions.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> treating a regulation as a convention; treating a convention as a law; confusing a rebuilt design with a better one without evidence.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> an expense-management product's three-week onboarding is decomposed into fixed facts (employee data, a policy, a verified bank connection) and conventions (live training before use, full policy configuration up front). The rebuild defers training until after the first reimbursement run. See Worked example 2.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"fw-c02-03\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C02-03 · The tradeoff table · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> two to four options; the goal; the constraints (time, people, risk).</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a table recording, for each option, what it optimizes, what it costs and who bears the cost, how reversible it is, and what evidence it needs; plus a recommendation with the cost stated.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the options are genuinely different in kind; the criteria come from the goal, not from habit.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> clarity versus the time needed to fill it in honestly; a table can imply precision that is not there.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> an option column with no costs; criteria added after the favorite is chosen; weighting that hides the real sacrifice.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> for a payments app, three candidate launches are compared on speed, risk to data integrity and reversibility; the table shows that the fastest option's cost falls on customers' money, which rules it out. See Worked example 3.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs (and who bears it)</th>\n<th>Reversibility</th>\n<th>Evidence needed</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Do nothing</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Recommendation line:</strong> &quot;I recommend B, which gives us ___ by giving up ___. This is [reversible / hard to reverse] because ___. I would change my mind if ___.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c02-04\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C02-04 · The decision classifier · Established</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Origin:</strong> one-way and two-way door decisions, as described in Amazon's shareholder letters in the mid-2010s <code>[V: Bezos 2015 shareholder letter (published April 2016), repeated in the 2016 letter]</code>; cost of delay as an economic input to product decisions, from Donald Reinertsen, <em>The Principles of Product Development Flow</em> (2009). Combining them into one three-question classifier is this book's arrangement.</li>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a decision; estimates of how reversible it is, what an error costs, and what waiting costs.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> one of three modes: decide now with light evidence; decide with evidence and a plan to verify; stage the decision (pilot, flag, notice period) so it becomes reversible.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you can estimate the three quantities roughly; reversibility is judged for all affected audiences, not only for engineering.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a quick classification costs minutes, and misclassifying a one-way decision as reversible is costly.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> classifying by how hard the change is to code rather than how hard it is to undo for users; treating cost of delay as the only input; never revisiting the class when facts change.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> three decisions in one week at a payments app (a message wording, a refund-window change, a legacy API shutdown) land in three different modes. See Worked example 3.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Reversible?</th>\n<th>Error costly?</th>\n<th>Waiting costly?</th>\n<th>Mode</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Any</td>\n<td>Decide now; test if cheap</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Decide now, <a href=\"#gl-staged-rollout\">staged rollout</a> with a rollback trigger</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Decide slowly with evidence; stage or give notice</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Reduce irreversibility first (pilot, flag), then decide</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c02-05\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C02-05 · The pre-mortem · Established</h5>\n<p><strong>Core framework.</strong> Learn this one first.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Origin:</strong> Gary Klein, &quot;Performing a Project Premortem,&quot; <em>Harvard Business Review</em>, September 2007.</li>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a plan or launch about to be committed; the people who will execute it.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a ranked list of reasons the plan could fail, with mitigations, monitoring signals and, where needed, changes to scope or launch criteria.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> participants will state concerns when asked to imagine failure; someone owns acting on the list.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> thirty to sixty minutes of team time versus catching a failure before it is expensive; it can surface fear that stalls a team if not closed with decisions.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> treating it as a risk-register ritual; letting the most senior voice set the list; producing risks with no owner or action.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> a family location-sharing feature is imagined to have failed six months after launch; the failure list surfaces misuse, teen workarounds and privacy backlash, and changes the launch criteria. See Worked example 4.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Steps: (1) tell the team the launch has failed, spectacularly, six months from now; (2) each person writes reasons independently; (3) collect and cluster; (4) rank by likelihood and impact; (5) for each top item decide a mitigation, a signal and an owner; (6) change the plan.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c02-06\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C02-06 · The decision memo · Original</h5>\n<p><strong>Core framework.</strong> Learn this one first.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a decision that needs agreement or a record; the evidence gathered.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a one- to two-page document: decision, context, options, criteria, recommendation, tradeoffs accepted, reversibility, what would change the recommendation, owner and date.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> readers will read a short document and the decision owner will act on it.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> writing time versus repeated meetings; a memo can freeze a decision that should stay open.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> burying the recommendation; options written as straw men; no statement of what would change the decision.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the refund-window decision from Worked example 3 written as a memo, ending with &quot;revisit if refund-fraud cost exceeds the threshold for two consecutive months (invented).&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Template, in the order a reader needs it:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Decision needed and by when.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Recommendation</strong> (one sentence, with the main cost).</li>\n<li><strong>Context</strong> (what changed; why now).</li>\n<li><strong>Options considered</strong> (including doing nothing), each with what it optimizes and costs.</li>\n<li><strong>Criteria</strong> and how each option scores.</li>\n<li><strong>Reversibility and the plan if we are wrong.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>What would change this recommendation.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Owner, next step, review date.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c02-4-answer-architecture\"></span>\n<h3>4. Answer architecture</h3>\n<p>Two question types use this chapter directly. The same structure carries over, with additions, to the design and execution rounds covered in C07, C15 and C17.</p>\n<span id=\"c02-type-a-the-tradeoff-or-should-we-build-it-question\"></span>\n<h4>Type A: the tradeoff or &quot;should we build it&quot; question</h4>\n<p><em>Example prompts:</em> &quot;A feature could ship a week early with minor bugs. Do you ship?&quot; &quot;Sales wants a custom report for one large customer. Do you build it?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Structure</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Restate</strong> the decision and who decides.</li>\n<li><strong>Clarify</strong> the goal, the constraints, the audience affected, the timeline, and how reversible the decision is.</li>\n<li><strong>Frame the options</strong>, including a smaller version and doing nothing.</li>\n<li><strong>Set criteria</strong> drawn from the goal (and say which criterion matters most).</li>\n<li><strong>Compare</strong> with tradeoffs named in the sacrifice sentence.</li>\n<li><strong>Classify</strong> reversibility and cost of delay; choose the mode (FW-C02-04).</li>\n<li><strong>Recommend</strong>, state the cost, and say what would change your mind.</li>\n<li><strong>Close</strong> with the first step and how you would know early if you were wrong.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Time budget for a ten-minute segment inside a 45-minute round</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Minutes</th>\n<th>What to produce</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Restate and clarify</td>\n<td>1.5</td>\n<td>Goal, constraints, reversibility, who decides</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Frame options</td>\n<td>1.5</td>\n<td>Two to four options of different kinds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Criteria and compare</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Named tradeoffs; the deciding criterion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Classify and recommend</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Mode, recommendation, stated cost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Close</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>What would change your mind; first step; early signal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Slack for interviewer questions</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>What to clarify first.</strong> The goal behind the decision, the people affected, what has already been committed, how soon the answer is needed, and what happens if the decision is wrong.</p>\n<p><strong>Where to signpost.</strong> After clarifying: &quot;I will compare three options against the goal, then recommend.&quot; Before the recommendation: &quot;My deciding criterion is X.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Where to state tradeoffs.</strong> In the comparison and again in the recommendation line. The interviewer should hear the sacrifice twice.</p>\n<p><strong>How to close.</strong> One sentence: &quot;I recommend B, which gets us ___ by giving up ___; it is reversible through ___; I would switch if ___.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c02-type-b-walk-me-through-a-decision-you-made-and-why-probes\"></span>\n<h4>Type B: &quot;Walk me through a decision you made&quot; and why-probes</h4>\n<p>This is the behavioral form of the same skill and is covered in full in C31. The decision-story structure is: context and goal, options you considered, the criteria, the decision and its cost, the result, and what you would do differently. When an interviewer probes (&quot;why not the other option?&quot;), answer with the criterion that decided it and the evidence you had at the time, not with the outcome.</p>\n<span id=\"c02-type-c-the-why-probe-in-any-round\"></span>\n<h4>Type C: the why-probe in any round</h4>\n<p>When the interviewer says &quot;why?&quot;, resist defending; respond with the reasoning in three parts: the goal you were serving, the option you rejected and its cost, and the evidence that would have changed your answer. This shows that your preference follows from reasoning rather than the other way round.</p>\n<span id=\"c02-5-worked-examples\"></span>\n<h3>5. Worked examples</h3>\n<p>All four examples are <code>Illustrative</code>: the products are fictional and every number is invented.</p>\n<span id=\"c02-worked-example-1-consumer-the-pause-subscription-request-ill\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 1 — Consumer: the &quot;pause subscription&quot; request (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>Setting.</strong> A meal-kit app receives about 140 support tickets a month (invented) asking for a way to pause. A sales lead says: &quot;Add a pause button.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Step 1: restate the request as a need.</strong> The people asking want to stop receiving boxes for a while without losing their account and preferences. That is the need. &quot;Pause button&quot; is one way to meet it.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2: find the segment and the situation.</strong> A sample of 60 tickets (invented) gives travel 24, budget 15, &quot;too many boxes this week&quot; 12, other 9. The concentration is in subscribers whose availability varies week to week. A quarter of last quarter's cancellations (invented) came from subscribers who had asked support about skipping a week in the prior 14 days.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3: write the outcome.</strong> Raise 60-day retention for subscribers who ask about skipping. That is a <a href=\"#gl-metric\">metric</a> with a segment, a direction and a window.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4: generate options of different kinds.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n<th>Reversibility</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Pause up to three months</td>\n<td>Widest coverage of absences</td>\n<td>Pausers may drift into silent churn; billing and inventory complexity</td>\n<td>Hard once subscribers rely on it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Self-serve &quot;skip a week&quot; with a cutoff reminder</td>\n<td>The stated cause (uneven schedules)</td>\n<td>Does not help long absences</td>\n<td>High; behind a <a href=\"#gl-feature-flag\">feature flag</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Flexible delivery day</td>\n<td>Fewer skips needed</td>\n<td>Logistics cost</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D. Do nothing; give support a skip macro</td>\n<td>No build cost</td>\n<td>Support load stays; no evidence of demand</td>\n<td>High</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Step 5: evidence plan and decision.</strong> Ship B to 20% of the segment and compare 60-day retention with the rest. Add a <a href=\"#gl-counter-metric\">counter metric</a>: revenue per subscriber, so that skipping is not just a quieter way to lose revenue. Cost of delay is modest (140 tickets at an assumed $6 handling cost is $840 a month, invented, plus cancellations), so a short test is justified. Revisit A if B adoption is high and exit surveys still cite long absences.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The request named a solution. The chain turned it into a problem, produced a smaller reversible option, and defined how to learn. Note the check on the survey evidence: ticket reasons are what people say, so the plan also looks at what they do (the <a href=\"#gl-say-do-gap\">say-do gap</a>, C06).</p>\n<span id=\"c02-worked-example-2-b2b-rebuilding-onboarding-from-first-princi\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 2 — B2B: rebuilding onboarding from first principles (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>Setting.</strong> An expense-management product takes a median of 21 days (invented) for a new customer to complete its first reimbursement run. The team's plan is a better training deck.</p>\n<p><strong>Decompose, then label each step as fixed or convention.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Days (invented)</th>\n<th>Fixed fact or convention?</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Collect employee list and approvers by spreadsheet</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Data is needed (fixed); the spreadsheet is a convention</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Configure the full expense policy</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>At least one rule is needed (fixed); configuring everything up front is a convention</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Connect and verify the bank account for payouts</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Bank verification is required <code>[as of 2026-10; varies by market]</code> (treated as fixed)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Live admin training sessions</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Convention</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Custom report setup</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Convention</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Rebuild.</strong> The goal is &quot;a finance admin completes a first reimbursement run.&quot; From the fixed facts alone: sync employees from the company's directory instead of a spreadsheet (one day); apply a default policy template that the admin can edit later (same day); start bank verification on day one in parallel; teach through in-product guidance after the first run; defer reports. The critical path becomes the five-day bank verification plus one day, so about six days (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Check the rebuild.</strong> What could break? Default policies may misfit some companies, so show the defaults for approval before the first run. Directory sync may not exist for small customers, so keep a spreadsheet fallback.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A training deck would have improved a convention. Decomposition showed that most of the 21 days was convention, and that the one real floor, bank verification, should start first. It also shows where first principles stops: the five-day step did not go away.</p>\n<span id=\"c02-worked-example-3-fintech-classifying-three-decisions-in-one-\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 3 — Fintech: classifying three decisions in one week (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>Setting.</strong> A payments app's PM faces three decisions this week.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Decision</th>\n<th>Reversible?</th>\n<th>Error costly?</th>\n<th>Waiting costly?</th>\n<th>Mode (FW-C02-04)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>a. Reword the failed-payment message</td>\n<td>Yes, in minutes</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Decide today; test if cheap</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>b. Cut the refund window from 7 days to 3</td>\n<td>Partly: customers will have seen the policy</td>\n<td>High: trust and possible rule constraints <code>[varies by market]</code></td>\n<td>Moderate: refund losses of 12k a month (invented)</td>\n<td>Stage the decision</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>c. Retire the legacy API used by 40 merchants (invented)</td>\n<td>No: merchants must migrate</td>\n<td>High: broken payments</td>\n<td>Moderate: upkeep ties up two engineers (invented)</td>\n<td>Decide with evidence; give notice</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Decision b in detail, using the tradeoff table.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs and who bears them</th>\n<th>Reversibility</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Keep 7 days</td>\n<td>Customer goodwill</td>\n<td>Refund losses continue (business)</td>\n<td>Full</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cut to 3 days for everyone</td>\n<td>Loss reduction</td>\n<td>Trust cost to existing customers</td>\n<td>Hard once announced</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cut to 3 days for new customers only</td>\n<td>Loss reduction with less trust risk</td>\n<td>Two policies to explain and operate</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tier by risk score</td>\n<td>Targets the loss</td>\n<td>Fairness and explanation burden; needs engineering</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Recommendation line.</strong> &quot;I recommend 3 days for new customers only, which cuts refund losses over time by giving up some policy simplicity. It is reversible through a review date, and I would extend it to existing customers if refund losses stay above the threshold for two consecutive months (invented) and complaint volume stays flat.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Decision c.</strong> Publish a deprecation date with a long notice period, a migration guide, and a named contact, and measure how many merchants have migrated at each checkpoint. The irreversibility sits with merchants, so the process, not the speed, carries the weight.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Classification took minutes and produced three different speeds. Decision (a) would have been a mistake to over-process, and (c) a mistake to rush.</p>\n<span id=\"c02-worked-example-4-consumer-safety-a-pre-mortem-for-family-loc\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 4 — Consumer safety: a pre-mortem for family location sharing (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>Setting.</strong> A family app plans to launch location sharing between family members in six weeks.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 1: declare failure.</strong> &quot;It is six months after launch and the feature has been pulled. Why?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2: independent lists, then cluster.</strong> The team's lists cluster into five groups; the ratings are judgments made up for this example (invented):</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Cluster</th>\n<th>Example reasons</th>\n<th>Likelihood</th>\n<th>Impact</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Misuse</td>\n<td>Sharing used to monitor someone who did not freely agree</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Severe</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Teen workarounds</td>\n<td>Teens turn off permissions, trust drops</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Privacy backlash</td>\n<td>A press story about location data handling</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>High</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reliability</td>\n<td>Battery drain and stale locations</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>False reassurance</td>\n<td>Parents assume the map is always current</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>High</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Step 3: mitigations, signals and owners for the top clusters.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Misuse: explicit opt-in from each person, a persistent visible sharing indicator, one-tap pause, and safety resources linked from the settings screen. Owner: product and trust and safety. Signal: reports of sharing without consent.</li>\n<li>Privacy: minimize retention, publish what is stored and for how long, and review with privacy counsel before launch. Owner: product and legal.</li>\n<li>False reassurance: show &quot;last updated&quot; time on every location. Owner: design.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Step 4: change the plan.</strong> The pre-mortem changed three launch criteria: opt-in from both sides, a visible indicator, and a kill criterion (&quot;pause the launch if verified consent-violation reports exceed a set threshold in the first four weeks&quot;). It also moved launch from all users to a <a href=\"#gl-staged-rollout\">staged rollout</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The pre-mortem made a vague worry specific enough to act on, and it turned a design discussion into launch criteria. The design depth for this kind of feature is in the responsible-design check of C07.</p>\n<span id=\"c02-6-baseline-strong-standout\"></span>\n<h3>6. Baseline, Strong, Standout</h3>\n<p><strong>Prompt:</strong> &quot;A new feature can ship in two weeks with known rough edges, or in six weeks fully polished. What do you do?&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c02-baseline-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Baseline answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;I'd probably ship it in two weeks, because speed matters and we can iterate. Users prefer getting features early, and we can fix the rough edges later. I'd tell the team to prioritize the most important bugs. If it's really bad I'd wait.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>What is wrong:</em> no goal, no definition of &quot;rough edges,&quot; no cost named, no reversibility, no evidence plan. &quot;If it's really bad I'd wait&quot; defers the whole decision.</p>\n<span id=\"c02-strong-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Strong answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;First I'd want to know the goal: is this a bet we are testing, or a commitment to customers? And what are the rough edges: cosmetic, or something that affects data or payments? Assuming the edges are minor and we can release behind a flag, I'd compare three options: ship in two weeks to everyone, ship in two weeks to 10% with a fix plan, or wait six weeks. Shipping to everyone gains learning speed but gives up polish for all users, and the cost lands on first impressions. Waiting gives up two to four weeks of learning. The staged option gains most of the learning while limiting the damage. I'd recommend the staged release, with a rule that anything touching data integrity blocks it. I'd measure task completion and support contacts for the 10%, and ship to everyone if they stay within a threshold we agree beforehand.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c02-standout-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Standout answer</h4>\n<p>Everything in the Strong answer, plus:</p>\n<p>&quot;Before choosing, I'd separate two things the question bundles together: how bad the rough edges are, and who sees them. If a six-week schedule is slow because of a review queue, I would try to fix the queue, since that makes the tradeoff go away. If it is real, my deciding criterion is reversibility: a flagged release is easy to roll back, so I should decide quickly, whereas a polished release delays learning on the question that matters most, which is whether people use the feature at all. The cost of delay is four weeks of that learning. I'd put the downside in writing: the rough edges give up first-session trust for 10% of users, which I'd offset by choosing the segment least likely to be harmed, such as opted-in beta users. I'd set the exit criteria first: a rollback trigger on error rate and a hold on expansion if completion falls below the control. And I'd say what would change my recommendation: if the feature is the first thing new customers see, or if the edges affect accessibility, I'd wait.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c02-what-moves-an-answer-up-a-band\"></span>\n<h4>What moves an answer up a band</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>From Baseline to Strong</th>\n<th>From Strong to Standout</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>States the goal and defines the rough edges</td>\n<td>Separates the bundled variables (severity, audience, cause of the delay)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Offers more than two options, including a staged one</td>\n<td>Tests whether the tradeoff is real before accepting it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Names the cost in a sacrifice sentence</td>\n<td>Names who bears the cost and offsets it deliberately</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chooses a rollout mode and a decision rule</td>\n<td>Sets exit criteria before launch and says what would reverse the recommendation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These map directly to signals 1 to 5 in section 1.</p>\n<span id=\"c02-7-company-lens\"></span>\n<h3>7. Company lens</h3>\n<p>All claims below are about what companies say publicly or what candidates commonly report; none is insider knowledge. Interview formats change <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Google [as of 2026-10].</strong> Google publicly describes hiring on general cognitive ability, role-related knowledge, leadership and &quot;Googleyness&quot; <code>Public</code> <code>[V: summaries citing Google's how-we-hire page; Google re:Work structured-interviewing guide]</code>. Reported APM loops have rounds on product sense, execution and estimation, where a candidate who chooses and defends is distinguished from one who lists options <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: end tradeoff answers with a recommendation and the sentence that names the cost.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Amazon [as of 2026-10].</strong> Amazon publishes its Leadership Principles, including Are Right, A Lot, Bias for Action and Dive Deep <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Amazon's Leadership Principles page, amazon.jobs]</code>. Its shareholder letters in the mid-2010s describe one-way and two-way door decisions <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Bezos 2015 shareholder letter (published April 2016), repeated in the 2016 letter, as quoted in AWS and secondary material]</code>, and its principle Have Backbone; Disagree and Commit covers speaking up and then committing <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Amazon's Leadership Principles page, amazon.jobs]</code>. Using reversibility language is likely to be well received in an Amazon-style loop <code>Inferred</code>, as long as it is backed by a concrete classification.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Meta [as of 2026-10].</strong> Candidates commonly report product sense, execution and leadership rounds, with probing on metrics and tradeoffs <code>Inferred</code> <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. Expect follow-ups of the form &quot;what would you measure to know it worked, and what could that metric hide?&quot;, which is where the second-order and counter-metric habits pay off.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Microsoft [as of 2026-10].</strong> Microsoft publicly emphasizes a growth-mindset culture <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Microsoft careers culture page]</code>. Reports suggest interviewers value candidates who say what they would learn from being wrong <code>Inferred</code>. Include the &quot;what would change my mind&quot; sentence and, in behavioral answers, what you changed afterward.</p>\n</blockquote>\n";
